  [1mB. Append-only event log, derive feed on read[0m — task changes append to [38;5;153mtask_events[39m; each user's feed is a query
  joining events against their subscriptions and a [38;5;153mlast_read_at[39m cursor. You get task history for free, and watch/mute
  apply retroactively. Cost: per-notification read/dismiss state needs its own receipts table, and feed queries get
  heavier as events accumulate. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mC. Event log plus materialized fanout[0m — B as the source of truth, A as a cache. Most robust and the only one that
  survives changing the fanout rules after the fact. Complexity: high, and it's two systems to keep consistent.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m In-app-only with modest scale means the read-path performance argument for B never bites, and B's
  headline win — retroactive history — is a feature you didn't ask for. A's real weakness is that fanout rules are baked
  into stored rows; the mitigation is keeping subscriber resolution in one pure function ([38;5;153mresolveRecipients(task, [39m
  [38;5;153mevent)[39m) so it's independently testable and swappable. C is what you'd migrate to if an activity feed ever becomes a
  product requirement, and A doesn't block that.

  [1mStack and tooling[0m

  The repo has no tooling at all, so this is free to decide now and annoying later. I'd propose Node with Fastify and
  SQLite (single file, zero setup, real SQL), a plain-JS frontend with [38;5;153mfetch[39m against a JSON API — matching the existing
  hand-written HTML rather than importing a framework for two screens — and ESLint plus Prettier from the first commit.

  Separately, the testing question. This one is worth your input rather than my assumption:

